ARM: dts: pyra: enable the ipu and dsp

Carveouts and enablement for the Pyra V5.3 board, modelled on
omap5-uevm.dts, which is the closest upstream board and the one this
machine already claims compatibility with: /proc/device-tree/compatible
reads "goldelico,lc15-v5.3 goldelico,lc15 ti,omap5-uevm ti,omap5".

Sizes and addresses are upstream's: 8 MB of dsp carveout at 0x95000000
and 56 MB of ipu carveout at 0x95800000, both reusable, so the pages go
back to the page allocator whenever the processor they belong to is
stopped.  The Pyra has 4 GB, of which 2.8 GB was free on the unit this
was written against, so both fit without taking anything from CMA, which
stays at its 64 MB.

What is NOT copied from omap5-uevm.dts is the timers.  Upstream gives
the ipu ti,timers = <&timer3> and ti,watchdog-timers = <&timer9>,
<&timer11>, and the dsp timer5 and timer6.  timer9 on a Pyra is the
display backlight PWM -- pyra-display.dtsi declares pwm9 on it and the
panel's backlight node consumes it.  timer8 is the keyboard backlight
for the same reason.

The conflict fails safe, and it is worth stating which way round it goes.
pwm-omap-dmtimer requests its timer in probe and frees it in remove, and
CONFIG_PWM_OMAP_DMTIMER=y here, so the backlight reserves timer9 during
boot and holds it.  omap_rproc_enable_timers is reached only from
omap_rproc_start, never from probe, so the remoteproc driver asks much
later and _omap_dm_timer_request skips a reserved timer.  The backlight
therefore wins, the display is unaffected, and what breaks is starting
the processor: request_by_node returns NULL and
omap_rproc_request_timer returns -EBUSY.  A -EBUSY at start with no
apparent connection to a backlight is a bad hour, which is the actual
reason to leave these out rather than any risk to the panel.

Both properties are optional.  omap_rproc_probe counts them with
of_count_phandle_with_args and clamps a negative count to zero, and the
watchdog property is only looked at under CONFIG_OMAP_REMOTEPROC_WATCHDOG
at all, so leaving them out costs nothing until firmware needs a tick
source.  When something does, timer3, timer4, timer10 and timer11 are
all unclaimed on this board.

Origin: shaped after v6.6 arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap5-uevm.dts.
See docs/m4-ipu.md.

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/omap5-letux-cortex15-v5.3+pyra-v5.3.dts	2026-09-20 14:06:13.726043527 -0400
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/omap5-letux-cortex15-v5.3+pyra-v5.3.dts	2026-09-20 14:06:13.780934686 -0400
@@ -23,3 +23,48 @@
 	pwms = <&pwm9 0 (1000000000 / 8300) 0>;
 };
 
+
+/*
+ * The two Cortex-M4s (ipu) and the C66x (dsp). Both are held in reset
+ * until remoteproc loads firmware into them; see docs/m4-ipu.md.
+ *
+ * No ti,timers or ti,watchdog-timers here, deliberately. omap5-uevm.dts
+ * gives the ipu timer9 and timer11 and the dsp timer5 and timer6, and
+ * timer9 on a Pyra is the display backlight PWM (pyra-display.dtsi) --
+ * timer8 is the keyboard one. Both properties are optional: the driver
+ * counts them with of_count_phandle_with_args and treats a missing
+ * property as zero timers. Firmware that wants a tick source of its own
+ * can be given timer3, timer4, timer10 or timer11, all of which are
+ * free here.
+ */
+/ {
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <2>;
+		#size-cells = <2>;
+		ranges;
+
+		dsp_memory_region: dsp-memory@95000000 {
+			compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
+			reg = <0 0x95000000 0 0x800000>;
+			reusable;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
+		ipu_memory_region: ipu-memory@95800000 {
+			compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
+			reg = <0 0x95800000 0 0x3800000>;
+			reusable;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&ipu {
+	status = "okay";
+	memory-region = <&ipu_memory_region>;
+};
+
+&dsp {
+	status = "okay";
+	memory-region = <&dsp_memory_region>;
+};
